            <div xml:id="d113-a48">

                <pb facs="0297" n="225" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg225"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 47 in No. 331.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Dawson to Mr.
                            Lowell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Consulate of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Queenstown</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-02-23"><hi rendition="#italic">February</hi> 23,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inclose a copy of a
                    letter received to-day from Mr. Michael Hart.</p>
                <p>On Sunday last Mr. Hart, of Carrigtohill, brother of Michael Hart, called on me.
                    I Informed him of the fact of my having had an interview with the chief of
                    police at Mi die ton, and that I was under the impression his brother would be
                    released provided he would sign the undertaking which I advised him to
                    offer.</p>
                <p>From what I could gather, Michael Hart is not disposed to sign any such
                    undertaking. He evidently values the opinions of his friends in this country
                    more than his liberty, and he also “thinks it would be cowardly to sign any
                    pledge for his future conduct or to accept release, except unconditionally.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer><signed>G. B. DAWSON,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Deputy and
                            Vice-Consul.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
